A||||12||mindscape||Rebuilt Engine Noise||||5||
Z||000000||mindscape||08-09-2001||04:12 AM||||I recently rebuilt my 88 GT 2.8 as a 3.1 AND have develped a clanking noise after the first 550 or so miles.  What is does NOT sound like is a pecking valve/lifter.  It is difficult to describe.  It does change with engine RPM; gets louder.  I don't think it is a bad rocker arm, though that is where the noise sounds like it is coming from; sounds more like a lifter slamming a cam lobe, but I'm not sure - it's a noise I have not dealt with before.<P>Valve lash was adjusted with 1/2 turn as recommended by the fellow who walked me through the rebuild.  Too loose?  That's what the fellow who machined the heads said it sounded like, or maybe a bad stud.<P>During the turn-in period I'm using Mobil 10W30; changed at 250 and at 550.<P>Other information... I'm using a Melling performance cam MTC-5 and matching lifters.  The heads were setup for use with this cam; new valve springs (VS-380) with an installed height of 1.72"<P>This is just TOO weird...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Did I miss something?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Hank is Here||08-09-2001||05:56 AM||||You said you were using Mobil1 10-30 as break-in oil.  Is this synthetic or natural?  Everything I have read says use natural oil as the break-in oil then switch to the synthetic.  I know that probably is not the problem but.  <BR>Okay try taking a long rachet extension end using it as a stethoscope to better isolate the noise to an area of the engine. <P>Hank ||||reg||1
Z||000002||ironhedno3||08-09-2001||12:16 PM||||hope your not using synthetic as a break in oil..is the crank hitting the oil pick up tube?||||reg||6
Z||000003||mindscape||08-09-2001||12:33 PM||||Not using synthetic as break in oil.  Crank is not hitting pickup tube.  Noise is in top of the engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||08-09-2001||12:55 PM||||Well if your sure the noise is in the top of the motor It sounds like the valves have to be readjusted at the very least.since you know where the noise is coming from open up the suspect valve cover and have look inside should be easy to see if you have a loose rocker arm in there from what you describe.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mindscape||08-09-2001||03:25 PM||||I've readjusted the rockers.  Before it is taken apart again I want to make a list of things to check.  Like, look for a loose/broken stud.  The noise is more in the cam location than rocker arm area.||||reg||1
Z||000006||starfighter007@msn.com||08-09-2001||04:13 PM||||your on the right track as what to look for.But Guess there is no better way then to get in there and see what you find.I doubt broken stud . I am thinking loose rocker arm still. I dont know what else to tell you until we get the cover off.keep us posted||||reg||1
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||08-09-2001||04:16 PM||||You know I just re read your post again.I wonder if it could be a bad fuel injector?Hmmm.||||reg||3
Z||000008||mindscape||08-09-2001||07:00 PM||||I replaced the injectors the other night with another set when I took it apart to check the rocker arms and push-tubes.  I don't believe that I have a bad lifter or bad wrist pin.  But who knows?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  There are metal flakes in the oil ... so something is BAD.  When the engine went bad it ate the cam gear; why I don't know.||||reg||1
Z||000009||starfighter007@msn.com||08-09-2001||07:33 PM||||What? I am really confused ? what do you mean you found metal flakes .and maybe its the cam gear. Did you run the motor with the avalve cover off? If so was the ticking noise coming from a rocker arm? Where are these metal flakes? ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||08-09-2001||08:49 PM||||When you did the rebuild, did you get the block cleaned out?||||reg||1
Z||000011||mindscape||08-09-2001||11:15 PM||||There is metal in the oil - on the dipstick.  I'll know more in a few days when I take it apart; going to drain the oil and examine.<P>When was it rebuilt.  Recently, only a few weeks old.  Yes, the block was cleaned, allegedly when the cylinders were bored .030" over; had a nasty scratch on number one.  Crankshaft from Crankshaft Rebuilders .010" turned.<P>Took it over to Redline Performance center near Bristol Motor Speedway and the fellow swears it is a BAD lifter.  Funny, I've never heard one make this kind of noise.  Wish there was someway you guys could hear it.||||reg||1
